preConditionHook:
// ARM THE BREAKEVEN STOP STRUCTURALLY, ON THE TRADE'S OWN PROGRESS. Once
// favorableCloses CONSECUTIVE closes since entry have each moved in the trade's
// favour -- close[0] > close[1] for a long -- the breakeven stop is armed, and
// a later close back at the entry price takes the trade off. Grimes: "once a
// trade is working ... tighten stops to reduce open risk." His worked example
// arms on 2 closes.
//
// BreakevenStopAfterEmaHoldingRun is the shape and the latch idiom, copied term
// for term; only the ARMING TEST differs. The two arm on completely different
// bars, and no input setting of either reproduces the other, so this is a
// variant and not a parameterization of it. Kept apart from BreakevenStop,
// whose trigger is peak profit reaching a dollar figure.
//
// THE COUNTERS AND THE ARMED FLAGS ARE PER POSITION and reset on
// ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0 -- VERIFIED as 0 on the fill bar and as restarting
// through a REVERSAL, which a MarketPosition() == 0 reset would miss. While
// flat the reset repeats. The counter advances on the fill bar too, so a fill
// bar closing above the previous close is the first of the run.
//
// close[0] > close[1] and close[0] <= EntryPrice are price DIFFERENCES, so the
// rule passes the shift test. All state is in localVariables, so the rule
// survives double placement.
if (ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0) {
    upRun = 0;
    dnRun = 0;
    armedLong = false;
    armedShort = false;
}
if (el_gt(close[0], close[1])) {
    upRun = upRun + 1;
} else {
    upRun = 0;
}
if (el_lt(close[0], close[1])) {
    dnRun = dnRun + 1;
} else {
    dnRun = 0;
}
if (el_ge((double)upRun, favorableCloses)) armedLong = true;
if (el_ge((double)dnRun, favorableCloses)) armedShort = true;
